## Colour roles in Home Assistant

These rules apply the functional colours to a Home Assistant interface.

<h3 id="bg-col-02">Choose a colour by its job</h3>

Give identity, reading, interaction, state and data their own colours. The theme colour marks navigation and actions. Text colours are for reading. Focus and error colours mark an interaction state. Device colours show a native device state. Chart colours name a source.

The Red theme colour is the same as the error colour (`#CA3535` in light mode and `#F0C6C6` in dark mode), and the Green theme colour is the same as the success colour (`#0F7A52`). With either theme colour, say an error or success in words: a green button is not proof that something worked, and a yellow lighting scene is not a warning. Choose a status fill and its text colour together, because a dark-mode error text colour is not always a safe button background.

This example lists 3 device checks. Notice that each state is written out, so none of them depends on its colour.

<%= example('bg-colour-state-labels') %>

<h3 id="bg-col-04">Separate dividers from editable boundaries</h3>

Use `civica-border-quiet` for dividers and quiet card edges: `#CECECE` in light mode, with a darker value in dark mode. Use `civica-border-interactive` for the edges of controls people need to find, and `civica-input-border` for fields people can type in. Keep the strong input and focus styles.

Do not outline every part of a dashboard in black: group content with headings and spacing first. Do not lighten every border either, because a text field, a focus ring and a card edge do different jobs. Keep native circle and pill shapes where they carry meaning, as on a switch, radio button, badge, avatar or gauge.

This example shows a card for renaming a room. Notice that the card edge is quiet, while the text field has a strong border and a clear focus ring.

<%= example('bg-colour-boundaries') %>

<h3 id="bg-col-05">Keep device, warning and notification meaning</h3>

Keep the native icon and state colours for a device, and keep the device name and state readable. An unavailable sensor is not a zero reading or a device that is off. A manufacturer's logo can help people recognise a device, but it is content, not part of the interface's identity.

The [notification badge](../components/notification-badge.html) keeps its fixed colour and rounded shape with every theme colour. Its count is not an alarm level, and a coloured dot does not replace a warning message. Give confirmed success, a pending action and a failure their own wording as well as their own look.
